LeetCode 103 — Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order Traversal

Given the root of a binary tree, return its level-order traversal, but alternate the direction at every level:

Level 1 → left → right
Level 2 → right → left
Level 3 → left → right
Level 4 → right → left
and so on.
Example
        3
       / \
      9   20
         /  \
        15   7

Normal level order:

[[3], [9,20], [15,7]]

Zigzag:

[[3], [20,9], [15,7]]

The important observation is that this is fundamentally a BFS / level-order traversal problem.

Approach 1 — Brute Force: Level-by-Level DFS

We can solve the problem without a queue by using DFS and storing nodes according to their depth.

Idea

During DFS:

root → level 0
children → level 1
grandchildren → level 2
...

For every node, add its value to the list corresponding to its level.

After collecting all levels:

Even level → keep order
Odd level → reverse

This is less direct than BFS because the problem is naturally level-based.

Code
class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        dfs(root, 0, result);

        for (int level = 0; level < result.size(); level++) {
            if (level % 2 == 1) {
                Collections.reverse(result.get(level));
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    public void dfs(TreeNode root, int level,
                    List<List<Integer>> result) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (level == result.size()) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }

        result.get(level).add(root.val);

        dfs(root.left, level + 1, result);
        dfs(root.right, level + 1, result);
    }
}

