Approach 3 — BFS + Add in Correct Direction Directly

The previous approach does unnecessary work:

Collections.reverse(list);

Instead of creating:

[9,20]

and then reversing it to:

[20,9]

we can construct the level in the required direction from the beginning.

Idea

Use an ArrayList and:

list.add(value);

for left-to-right.

For right-to-left:

list.add(0, value);

This avoids an explicit reverse().

Code
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        boolean leftToRight = true;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();
            List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode curr = q.poll();

                if (leftToRight) {
                    list.add(curr.val);
                } else {
                    list.add(0, curr.val);
                }

                if (curr.left != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }

                if (curr.right != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
            }

            result.add(list);

            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }

        return result;
    }
}

Important Problem

Although the code looks nice, this is not optimal in Java.

Why?

ArrayList.add(0, value) requires shifting existing elements.

For example:

[20,30,40]

Adding 10 at index 0 requires:

[20,30,40]
 ↓
[10,20,30,40]

Several elements have to move.

For a level containing k nodes, repeated front insertion can cost:

O(k²)

Therefore, in the worst case this approach can become:

O(n²)

So we should improve it.

Approach 4 — Optimal BFS Using Deque

A Deque allows insertion at both ends in O(1).

This makes it perfect for zigzag traversal.

Instead of:

list.add(0, curr.val);

we can use:

deque.addFirst(curr.val);

or:

deque.addLast(curr.val);

Both operations are O(1).

Optimal Code
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        boolean leftToRight = true;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();
            Deque<Integer> level = new ArrayDeque<>();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode curr = q.poll();

                if (leftToRight) {
                    level.addLast(curr.val);
                } else {
                    level.addFirst(curr.val);
                }

                if (curr.left != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }

                if (curr.right != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
            }

            result.add(new ArrayList<>(level));

            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }

        return result;
    }
}

Complexity

Every node is:

removed from queue once
added to deque once

Therefore:

Time = O(n)
Space = O(n)

This is asymptotically optimal because we must at least visit every node and output every value.

Approach 5 — BFS Using Array Indexing

We can also avoid both Collections.reverse() and front insertion.

At each level, allocate an array/list of exactly size and put each value directly at its correct position.

Idea

For a left-to-right level:

index = i

For a right-to-left level:

index = size - 1 - i
Code
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        boolean leftToRight = true;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();

            Integer[] level = new Integer[size];

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode curr = q.poll();

                int index;

                if (leftToRight) {
                    index = i;
                } else {
                    index = size - 1 - i;
                }

                level[index] = curr.val;

                if (curr.left != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }

                if (curr.right != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
            }

            result.add(Arrays.asList(level));

            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }

        return result;
    }
}

This is also:

Time = O(n)
Space = O(n)

