Why size = q.size() Is Critical

This is one of the most important concepts in level-order BFS.

Suppose:

        3
       / \
      9   20

Before processing level 2:

q = [9,20]

We store:

int size = q.size();

So:

size = 2

Then we process exactly 2 nodes.

While processing them, we may add children:

q = [children of 9, children of 20]

Those children belong to the next level, not the current level.

Therefore:

for(int i=0; i<size; i++)

ensures that we process only the nodes belonging to the current level.

Without size

If we simply used:

while(!q.isEmpty())

we would mix multiple levels together.

Why the Queue Stores Children Left-to-Right

Your code always does:

if(curr.left!=null){
    q.add(curr.left);
}

if(curr.right!=null){
    q.add(curr.right);
}

So the queue naturally maintains:

left child → right child

For example:

        3
       / \
      9   20

After processing 3:

Queue = [9,20]

Then we get:

[9,20]

For the zigzag direction, you simply reverse that level:

[20,9]

This is why your solution is so simple.

